g class='sig'>\u22120.10\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.18 to \u22120.03), about \u22121.6\u00b0F since 1874. Toledo's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.21\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.15 to +0.27), about +3.2\u00b0F since 1874. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1985, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.76\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.32 to +1.19) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.57\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.12 to +1.01). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; 5 months fell. Highs rose in 9 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Toledo has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Toledo's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.30 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.11 to +0.48), about +4.6 inches since 1871. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 35.0 inches a year. The wettest year was 2011 (48.76 in); the driest, 1894 (21.34 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 6 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}